snippet.textDisplay:
@cjames11 I get you 😄 it does hit that “nahhh that’s CGI” level at first glance.

What makes it believable is the tiny imperfections — slight timing delays, micro-corrections in the ankles, subtle shifts in weight. Real robots actually look more convincing when they’re not perfectly smooth. CGI tends to be too clean.

The wild part is how much of that human-like movement comes from real-time balance algorithms reacting hundreds of times per second. It’s less about copying humans and more about stabilizing physics.

