snippet.textDisplay:
@AnotherPerspective78 Ahh, I get exactly what you’re asking now—that’s a sharp observation.

The exposed metal leg is showing the raw mechanical motion from the actuators, which tends to look more rigid because you’re seeing the true joint timing and balance corrections. The covered leg, on the other hand, benefits from the outer shell and padding, which smooths out the visual motion and hides micro-adjustments happening underneath. It’s less about moving differently, and more about how much of the mechanics you can see.

I’m hoping they release a full demo with both legs exposed doing the same dance—that would answer a lot of questions. If they did, what specific movement would convince you it’s genuinely that fluid—turns, side steps, or full choreography?

