snippet.textDisplay:
@AnotherPerspective78 That’s a really fair question—and honestly, a lot of people noticed the same thing.

Covering most of the body while exposing one lower leg is usually done to show both sides at once: the human-like appearance and the real mechanical hardware underneath. It’s basically a visual “proof” that there are actual actuators and joints inside, not just a costume. At the same time, companies often keep parts covered because the outer shell also stabilizes cables, sensors, and balance systems that might look messy or unfinished on early prototypes.

I’m keeping an eye out for a full exposed demo, because that’s where the real truth shows. What would you personally want to see most to be convinced—full walking, running, or complex dancing with everything visible?

