snippet.textDisplay:
@irvangb94 That’s a really important distinction you pointed out. “AI robot” and “AI-generated video” mean completely different things, and mixing them up can definitely confuse people.

In this case, the robot uses AI for things like motion planning, balance, and adapting to its environment in real time—but the footage itself isn’t AI-generated. What’s fascinating is that newer humanoids are starting to use neural networks to learn movement from human demonstrations instead of relying only on pre-programmed steps, which is why they look more natural now.

Clarity matters a lot as this tech evolves, so I appreciate you bringing that up. As robots become more autonomous, what do you think will matter more to people—how real they look, or how intelligently they behave?

